[1mNAME[0m
     [1mdf[0m – display free disk space

[1mSYNOPSIS[0m
     [1mdf[0m [[1m--libxo[0m] [[1m-b[0m | [1m-g[0m | [1m-H[0m | [1m-h[0m | [1m-k[0m | [1m-m[0m | [1m-P[0m] [[1m-acIilntY[0m] [[1m-,[0m] [[1m-T[0m [4mtype[24m] [[4mfile[24m | [4mfilesystem[24m [4m...[24m]

[1mLEGACY[0m [1mSYNOPSIS[0m
     [1mdf[0m [[1m--libxo[0m] [[1m-b[0m | [1m-g[0m | [1m-H[0m | [1m-h[0m | [1m-k[0m | [1m-m[0m | [1m-P[0m] [[1m-acIilnY[0m] [[1m-,[0m] [[1m-T[0m [4mtype[24m] [[1m-t[0m [4mtype[24m] [[4mfile[24m | [4mfilesystem[24m [4m...[24m]

[1mDESCRIPTION[0m
     The [1mdf[0m utility displays statistics about the amount of free disk space on the specified mounted [4mfile[24m [4msystem[24m or on
     the file system of which [4mfile[24m is a part.  By default block counts are displayed with an assumed block size of 512
     bytes.  If neither a file or a file system operand is specified, statistics for all mounted file systems are
     displayed (subject to the [1m-t[0m option below).

     The following options are available:

     [1m--libxo[0m
             Generate output via libxo(3) in a selection of different human and machine readable formats.  See
             xo_parse_args(3) for details on command line arguments.

     [1m-a[0m      Show all mount points, including those that were mounted with the MNT_IGNORE flag.  This is implied for
             file systems specified on the command line.

     [1m-b[0m      Explicitly use 512 byte blocks, overriding any BLOCKSIZE specification from the environment.  This is the
             same as the [1m-P[0m option.  The [1m-k[0m option overrides this option.

:[K[K [KESCESC[KOO[KBB[K     [1m-c[0m      Display a grand total.
:[K[K [KESCESC[KOO[KBB[K
:[K[K [KESCESC[KOO[KBB[K     [1m-g[0m      Use 1073741824 byte (1 Gibibyte) blocks rather than the default.  This overrides any BLOCKSIZE
:[K[K [KESCESC[KOO[KBB[K             specification from the environment.
:[K[K [KESCESC[KOO[KBB[K
:[K[K [KESCESC[KOO[KBB[K     [1m-h[0m      “Human-readable” output.  Use unit suffixes: Byte, Kibibyte, Mebibyte, Gibibyte, Tebibyte and Pebibyte
:[K[K [KESCESC[KOO[KBB[K             (based on powers of 1024) in order to reduce the number of digits to four or fewer.  This applies to the
:[K[K [KESCESC[KOO[KBB[K             Size, Used, and Avail columns only; the iused and ifree columns will be displayed in powers of 1000.
:[K[K [KESCESC[KOO[KBB[K
:[K[K [KESCESC[KOO[KBB[K     [1m-H[0m, [1m--si[0m
:[K[K [KESCESC[KOO[KBB[K             Same as [1m-h[0m but based on powers of 1000.
:[K[K [KESCESC[KOO[KBB[K
